      "450 W rear motor with 900 W peak on most listings"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sources disagree on motor rating and top speed",
      "Still heavy for daily stair carrying",
      "Price hovers near $1,000"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The NIU KQi3 Max is a comfortable, long-range commuter with a larger battery than the Segway, at a slightly higher price and with some spec confusion to check on the listing."
  },
  {
    "id": "apollo-city",
    "name": "Apollo City (2025)",
    "badge": "Best for Performance",
    "category": "Dual Motor, 960 Wh, IP66",
    "description": "The current Apollo City is for riders who want real power and weather protection and can live with the weight. RiderGuide and ERideHero list the 2025 version with dual 1,000 W hub motors, a 960 Wh, 48 V battery, a 32 mph top speed and a 38-mile claimed range, and ERideHero measured roughly 30 miles in regular riding. It carries an IP66 rating, which is far more splash protection than most commuters, and suspension for rough streets. The cost is weight: about 65 pounds listed, so this is a roll-only scooter for most people. Be careful with older listings, because earlier Apollo City generations used a single 600 W motor, a 634 Wh battery and an IP54 rating at around 39 to 42 pounds. Make sure the scooter you buy is the generation you expect, and check whether your local laws allow its speed.",
    "pros": [
      "Dual 1,000 W motors and a 960 Wh battery, per RiderGuide and ERideHero",
      "IP66 water and dust rating on the current model",
      "Suspension for rough pavement",
      "32 mph top speed for open roads"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "About 65 lb, effectively roll-only",
      "Older generations share the name with very different specs",
      "Top speed exceeds many local limits"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The 2025 Apollo City is a powerful, well-sealed scooter for riders who want speed and hill-climbing, provided you confirm the generation and can manage 65 pounds."
  },
  {
    "id": "hiboy-s2-max",
