    "pros": [
      "Gotrax states compliance with UL 2272",
      "20 mph top speed confirmed by RiderGuide testing",
      "About 37 lb, manageable to carry",
      "Often under $500 on sale"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tested range of 14.6 miles vs 25 claimed",
      "Smaller battery than commuter picks",
      "Prices vary by color and retailer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "For short commutes on a budget, the Gotrax G4 is a credible, UL-referenced scooter; just plan around its real-world range of about 15 miles."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Electric Scooters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-electric-scooter/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best electric scooter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most commuters we would start with the Segway Ninebot Max G2, which Segway rates at 43 miles and 22 mph and Best Buy lists as UL 2272 certified. If you need to carry your scooter, the roughly 26-pound NIU KQi Air is easier to live with. On a budget, the Gotrax G4 covers short trips."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How far can an electric scooter really go?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect roughly 60 to 75 percent of the rated range in mixed city riding. ERideHero measured about 30 miles from the Segway Max G2, which is rated at 43, and RiderGuide measured 14.6 miles from the Gotrax G4, rated at 25. Heavier riders, hills, high speed and cold weather all reduce range."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does UL 2272 certification mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "UL 2272 is a safety standard for the electrical drive train, battery system and charger of personal e-mobility devices such as scooters and hoverboards. Certification means a test lab checked the design against fire and electrical hazards. The CPSC encourages buyers to choose certified devices."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I ride an electric scooter in the rain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if the maker allows it, and only lightly. An IPX5 or IP66 rating protects against splashes and spray, not submersion. Lower ratings like IPX4 are best kept to dry weather, and wet roads also reduce braking grip, especially on painted lines."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How heavy is a typical electric scooter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Lightweight models such as the NIU KQi Air are listed around 26 pounds. Mainstream commuters run from about 37 to 47 pounds, long-range commuters such as the Segway Max G2 are listed at 53.5 pounds, and dual-motor performance scooters often exceed 60 pounds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to charge an electric scooter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Very little. A 600 Wh battery takes a little over half a kilowatt-hour to fill once charger losses are included, which is about 10 cents at a typical US rate of 17 cents per kWh. Tires, brake pads and eventual battery replacement are the real running costs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is an electric scooter or an electric bike better for commuting?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Scooters are lighter, fold smaller and suit short urban trips with mixed transit. E-bikes are more stable, more comfortable over distance and better on rough roads. For commutes longer than about 10 miles each way, or for riders with balance concerns, an e-bike usually makes more sense."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Outdoor Gear",
    tags: [
      "best electric scooter",
      "electric scooter for adults",
      "commuter electric scooter",
      "long range electric scooter",
      "lightweight electric scooter",
      "ul 2272 electric scooter"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated for 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Electric Scooters</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Commuter, long-range, lightweight and budget scooters chosen on the numbers that decide daily use: real range, weight you can carry, water rating and certified batteries.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#segway-ninebot-max-g2"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="8.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#what-matters" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Actually Matters</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Picks</a>
          <a href="#range" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Rated vs Real Range</a>
          <a href="#weight-portability" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Weight and Portability</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Brakes, Tires and Battery Safety</a>
          <a href="#rules" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Laws, Speed and Where You Can Ride</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Charging, Maintenance and Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Who Should Skip a Scooter</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best electric scooter is the one you will actually carry up the stairs, trust in a light rain and plug in without worrying about the battery. Rated range and top speed sell scooters, but weight, water resistance and a certified battery decide whether you keep riding one after the first month.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not put these scooters through our own road tests. Instead, we started from the scooters that commuter-focused retailers and independent scooter testers keep returning to, then read the manufacturer spec sheets and retailer listings for each one: motor rating, battery capacity in watt-hours, rated range, top speed, weight, maximum rider load, water-resistance rating and battery certification. Where an independent site has published its own measured range or top speed, we say so and name the site.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted the picks toward what matters on a daily commute rather than headline speed. A scooter that weighs 65 pounds may be thrilling on a long straight, but it is a poor choice if you live on a third floor or need to lift it onto a train. A scooter with no meaningful water rating may be fine in a dry climate and a liability anywhere it rains. We also looked for evidence of battery safety certification, because a scooter is a large lithium-ion pack that you charge indoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices on scooters move constantly. The same model can sit at full price one month and a third off the next, and older generations are often cleared out at steep discounts. We mention list prices only where a retailer or the manufacturer publishes them, and you should treat every price on this page as a starting point for your own check rather than a promise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, scooter model names are messy. Regional versions of the same scooter can have different top speeds, and a newer generation often keeps an older name. Each product card tells you where the specs came from and flags known conflicts, so you can match the listing in front of you to the scooter we are describing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Matters When You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with battery capacity, not rated range. Capacity, measured in watt-hours (Wh), is the size of the fuel tank, and it is the most honest number on the spec sheet. Rated range depends on a test rider's weight, speed and the eco mode the maker chose, so two scooters with the same 40-mile claim can behave very differently. As a rough rule, a scooter with around 500 to 600 Wh will cover a typical urban round trip with room to spare, while packs under about 300 Wh suit short last-mile hops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, look at motor rating and hill performance. Most commuter scooters use a single rear or front hub motor rated somewhere between 250 and 500 watts nominal, with a higher peak figure for short bursts. Nominal power is what the motor can sustain, and it matters more than peak power if your route has long climbs. Dual-motor scooters climb far better but weigh much more and drain the battery faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check weight and fold. Every pound counts if you carry the scooter on stairs, into an office or onto public transport. The lightest serious commuters sit in the mid-20-pound range, mainstream models land between about 35 and 55 pounds, and performance scooters run well past 60. Look at how the stem latches when folded and whether the scooter can be rolled on its front wheel, because that changes how heavy it feels in practice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, look for a water-resistance rating and a safety certification. An IP rating such as IPX4, IPX5 or IP66 tells you how much spray the electronics are designed to handle; it is not a promise that riding through puddles is fine. In the US, UL 2272 is the safety standard for the electrical system of personal e-mobility devices, and UL 2271 covers light electric vehicle batteries. The Consumer Product Safety Commission has repeatedly urged buyers to choose devices certified to these standards.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Spec</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to look for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why it matters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery (Wh)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500 Wh or more for daily commuting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The most honest predictor of real range</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Motor (nominal)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">350 to 500 W for hills</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sustained power on climbs, not just bursts</td>
                  </tr>
