        <div id="range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Rated Range vs Real Range
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturer range figures are measured under kind conditions: a light rider, warm weather, flat ground and a steady speed well below the scooter's maximum, usually in the most economical riding mode. Segway, for example, publishes the test conditions behind its F2-series figures, including a 75 kg rider and an average of 16 km/h. Nobody commutes like that, which is why real range almost always comes in lower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Independent testers make the gap visible. ERideHero's published testing of the Segway Ninebot Max G2, which Segway rates at 43 miles, measured about 30 miles in regular riding and around 21 miles when ridden fast. RiderGuide's test of the Gotrax G4, rated at up to 25 miles, measured 14.6 miles. Those are not failures; they are what happens when a heavier rider uses the speed the scooter was sold on.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical rule is to plan on 60 to 75 percent of the rated range for mixed city riding, and less if you are heavy, ride at full speed, face hills, or ride in the cold. Lithium-ion batteries deliver noticeably less energy below about 50°F, so a commute that is comfortable in summer can become tight in winter. If your round trip is 12 miles, a scooter rated for 20 miles is cutting it fine; one rated for 35 to 40 gives you margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery age matters too. Packs gradually lose capacity with charge cycles, so a scooter that just covers your commute when new may fall short after a couple of years of daily use. Buying more battery than you need today is the simplest way to keep the scooter useful for longer, and it is the same logic we apply when sizing a <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station</a>: usable capacity always ends up lower than the label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-portability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Weight and Portability
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weight is the spec most buyers underrate. Rolling a 53-pound scooter along a flat sidewalk is easy; carrying it up three flights of stairs every evening is not. Before you buy, picture the hardest lift in your week, whether that is stairs, a car trunk or a train step, and choose a scooter you can manage on a tired day.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the light end, carbon-fiber and magnesium frames bring weight down sharply. NIU lists its KQi Air at 26.2 pounds and the full-carbon KQi Air X at 25.8 pounds, which is roughly half the weight of a long-range commuter. The trade-off is a smaller battery and a lower top speed. Mainstream commuters such as the Hiboy S2 Max, which Hiboy lists at 40 pounds, and the Gotrax G4, measured at about 37 pounds by independent testers, sit in the middle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-range commuters are heavier because batteries are heavy. The Segway Max G2 is listed at 53.5 pounds and the NIU KQi3 Max at roughly 44 to 47 pounds depending on the source. Performance scooters such as the current Apollo City, listed at around 65 pounds, are realistically roll-only machines for most riders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the fold, too. A secure latch, a stem that hooks to the deck and a scooter that can be wheeled while folded all make a heavy scooter easier to live with. Handlebars that fold down narrow the package for trunks and closets. Kickstands and rear fenders that double as carry points are small details that matter more than they appear on a spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Brakes, Tires and Battery Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brakes are the most important safety feature on any scooter. Most quality commuters pair a mechanical or hydraulic disc brake with an electronic or regenerative brake on the motor. Dual braking means you are not relying on one system, and regenerative braking recovers a small amount of energy on long descents. Drum brakes need less maintenance but usually stop less sharply than a well-adjusted disc.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tires decide comfort and grip. Pneumatic (air-filled) tires in the 10-inch range absorb road buzz and grip better in the wet, but they can puncture. Tubeless or self-sealing tires reduce flats; solid tires never go flat but ride harshly and lose grip on wet paint and metal. On a commuter without suspension, larger pneumatic tires do most of the comfort work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery safety deserves real attention. The Consumer Product Safety Commission has reported fires linked to micromobility batteries and urges buyers to choose devices certified to UL 2272, charge with the manufacturer's charger, avoid charging unattended or overnight near exits, and stop using a battery that has been damaged, swollen or soaked. Several of our picks list UL 2272 certification on their retail or manufacturer pages; we say so on each card, and you should confirm it on the listing for the exact model you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store and charge the scooter somewhere cool and dry, away from the only exit of a room, and do not leave it charging for days. If you also run lithium battery gear such as a <a href="/best-home-battery-backup-system/">home battery backup</a> or power station, the same habits apply. Our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 vs lithium-ion chemistry</a> covers why some battery types tolerate abuse better than others; most scooters use conventional lithium-ion cells for their weight advantage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Laws, Speed and Where You Can Ride
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric scooter rules vary by state and city, and they change often. Many places cap the speed scooters may travel, restrict riding on sidewalks, set a minimum rider age or require helmets for younger riders. Some cities allow scooters in bike lanes only. Before you buy a 30 mph scooter, check what your local rules actually allow, because the extra speed may be unusable on the routes you ride.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed also changes the risk. A fall at 15 mph is very different from a fall at 30. Higher-speed scooters need better brakes, larger tires, suspension and more rider skill, and they are less forgiving of potholes and wet road markings. For most commuters, a scooter that cruises comfortably in the high teens to low 20s is the sensible ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear a helmet every ride. A properly fitted bicycle helmet is the minimum; some riders of faster scooters choose helmets certified for higher-speed use. Add lights and reflective clothing for dusk and dark, and ride as if drivers cannot see you, because often they cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check transit and building rules. Some transit systems and apartment buildings restrict e-scooters or their batteries because of fire risk. Knowing those rules before you buy can save you from owning a scooter you cannot take where you need to go.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Charging, Maintenance and Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Electric scooters are cheap to run. Charging a 600 Wh battery from empty uses a little over half a kilowatt-hour once charger losses are included, which at a typical US rate of about 17 cents per kWh comes to roughly 10 cents per full charge. Even daily commuting adds only a few dollars a month to your power bill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is where the real costs show up. Pneumatic tires need checking every week or two; low pressure is the most common cause of flats and poor range. Brake pads wear and need adjusting, folding mechanisms loosen and need tightening, and bolts on the stem should be checked regularly. Tires and brake pads are the main consumables, and both are inexpensive if you can fit them yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            The battery is the most expensive part to replace. Treat it well: avoid storing it fully charged or fully empty for long periods, keep it out of extreme heat and cold, and charge it indoors at room temperature. Batteries generally last for hundreds of cycles before noticeable capacity loss, and our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/">how long lithium batteries last</a> explains the same aging pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty and parts support are worth paying for. Large brands such as Segway and NIU have established parts supply and service networks, which matters when a controller fails or a tire needs replacing. A no-name scooter that is cheap today can become scrap the first time a proprietary part breaks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip an Electric Scooter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A scooter is not the right answer for everyone. If your commute is longer than about 10 miles each way, involves fast traffic with no bike lanes, or crosses rough roads, an electric bike is usually safer and more comfortable. Bikes have larger wheels, a seated position and more stable handling. Our guide to the <a href="/best-electric-bike-for-seniors/">best electric bikes for seniors</a> covers step-through models that are easy to mount if balance or flexibility is a concern.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip a scooter, too, if you cannot store it indoors and charge it safely. Leaving a scooter outside exposes the battery and electronics to weather and theft, and charging in a crowded hallway is a fire risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Riders with balance problems, joint pain or limited grip strength should be cautious. Standing on a narrow deck for 20 minutes and absorbing road shocks through the hands is harder than it looks. A seated e-bike or e-trike may suit better, and if you have health concerns it is worth talking to your doctor before you start riding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if you mostly ride for fitness or recreation on trails, a scooter is the wrong tool. Commuter scooters are built for pavement, and their small wheels struggle on gravel and dirt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on Rated Range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rated range assumes a light rider, a warm day and eco mode. Independent tests routinely measure 60 to 75 percent of the claim. Compare battery watt-hours instead, and size for your longest round trip with at least a third in reserve, more if you ride through winter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy scooter is easy to roll and miserable to carry. If stairs, car trunks or trains are part of your routine, weight should be your first filter. A 26-pound scooter you carry happily beats a 55-pound one you dread lifting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Riding Through Rain on a Low IP Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                IPX4 means splash resistance, not waterproofing. Some makers, including Hiboy for the S2 Max, advise against riding in rain at all. Water damage to controllers and batteries is a common failure and often not covered by warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Charging With the Wrong Charger or Unattended</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Use the manufacturer's charger, charge somewhere you can see, and never leave a scooter charging overnight beside your only exit. The CPSC links many micromobility fires to damaged batteries and incompatible chargers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Tire Pressure Checks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Underinflated pneumatic tires puncture more easily, sap range and make handling vague. Check pressure every week or two with a gauge and inflate to the figure printed on the tire sidewall or in the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

