        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best electric scooter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most commuters we would start with the Segway Ninebot Max G2, which Segway rates at 43 miles and 22 mph and Best Buy lists as UL 2272 certified. If you need to carry your scooter, the roughly 26-pound NIU KQi Air is easier to live with. On a budget, the Gotrax G4 covers short trips.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far can an electric scooter really go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect roughly 60 to 75 percent of the rated range in mixed city riding. ERideHero measured about 30 miles from the Segway Max G2, which is rated at 43, and RiderGuide measured 14.6 miles from the Gotrax G4, rated at 25. Heavier riders, hills, high speed and cold weather all reduce range.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does UL 2272 certification mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                UL 2272 is a safety standard for the electrical drive train, battery system and charger of personal e-mobility devices such as scooters and hoverboards. Certification means a test lab checked the design against fire and electrical hazards. The CPSC encourages buyers to choose certified devices.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I ride an electric scooter in the rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the maker allows it, and only lightly. An IPX5 or IP66 rating protects against splashes and spray, not submersion. Lower ratings like IPX4 are best kept to dry weather, and wet roads also reduce braking grip, especially on painted lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy is a typical electric scooter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Lightweight models such as the NIU KQi Air are listed around 26 pounds. Mainstream commuters run from about 37 to 47 pounds, long-range commuters such as the Segway Max G2 are listed at 53.5 pounds, and dual-motor performance scooters often exceed 60 pounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to charge an electric scooter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very little. A 600 Wh battery takes a little over half a kilowatt-hour to fill once charger losses are included, which is about 10 cents at a typical US rate of 17 cents per kWh. Tires, brake pads and eventual battery replacement are the real running costs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an electric scooter or an electric bike better for commuting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Scooters are lighter, fold smaller and suit short urban trips with mixed transit. E-bikes are more stable, more comfortable over distance and better on rough roads. For commutes longer than about 10 miles each way, or for riders with balance concerns, an e-bike usually makes more sense.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

